  "Compendium": {
    "foundation": [
      {
        "id": "W19.PREMISE",
        "name": "Four factions, six settlements, one shared hobby",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "The atlas defines a connected reference region, not the entire planet. Four proposed civic factions, six settlement entries and their clubs share a sporting agreement while retaining different ordinary livelihoods, local priorities and construction traditions. Tinwater Junction, Cinderhouse and Saltglass Exchange are existing places. Crown Basin and Spanrest are new settlement proposals. New World City is the author-established championship destination, not an invented replacement capital or proof of a world government.\n\nThe Rivet Commons, Reedwater Compact, Lantern Exchange and Traverse Freeholds are new named factions. Their membership, politics and geography are proposals introduced here. Existing Wrangloids, breeders, carriers and publications are not retroactively owned by those factions. A high wave number does not elevate new fiction above the author’s premises.\n\nA faction can support benches, water, transport or a representative. It cannot change the properties of a purchased insect. A region can favor particular supply and racing traditions without every resident using the same build. A newcomer can still buy a complete tin and race locally; a trader can visit the world market without earning a championship berth.\n\nThe governing promise stays visible: personally assemble a living racer, enjoy its speed and identity, and try to win. The surrounding world supplies other complete pleasures—an exceptional capture, a useful brood, a paid piece of craft, a display, a memorable rival—not mandatory steps before the real game begins.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S01",
          "W19.S03"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.LAYERS",
        "name": "Geography, representation and supply are different maps",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "A settlement is a place with water, food, work and residents. A region is a useful travel/ecological grouping. A faction is a civic organization capable of maintaining agreements and backing representatives. A competition circuit is a set of recognized events. A supply route is a practical connection for people, stock, goods and information. Their boundaries can overlap without being identical.\n\nThe current atlas is topological. It states useful connections, receiving points and optional detours; it does not assert a compass bearing, distance, travel duration, country analogue or global climate. No route is priced by an invented number of kilometers. Broad environmental recovery and absolute scale are treated separately in the scope paper.\n\nThe sporting title applies to a declared discipline: locomotion plus equipment policy. Ground Open and Flyer Open share an Open construction principle but not one mixed race. Speedsters and Fireworks have their own corresponding ground or Flyer programmes where the venue can support them. A faction may fail to field one discipline without making that sport disappear from the world.\n\nThe four factions are the described signatories in this reference region, not a maximum faction count. The worked world-final format covers their four earned berths. Further signatories require a declared qualifying format and capacity; they do not appear as automatic empty podium places.",
        "refs": []
      }
    ],
    "regions": [
      {
        "id": "W19.REGION.TINWATER",
        "name": "Tinwater Junction — an attainable first bench",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Existing place refined.** Pump repair, reclaimed-water work and freight servicing support the settlement. Whitewashed interiors, patched enamel, readable shelves and clean working tanks contrast with worn industrial exteriors. It is one possible beginning, not the mandatory start location.\n\n**Local hobby.** Foundry Club remains a local cooperative venue. Mara Venn’s Two Nets still sells whole insects, prepared sects and coordinated projects; Jess Rill remains a pump attendant and catcher; Ivo Pell remains a freight clerk who prefers buying prepared stock. None receives an invented championship history. Nera Kest publishes only what Benchline has actually learned.\n\n**Sources.** Switchyard Verge, Split-sleeper bank, Ballast trench and Service culvert remain their existing habitats. Rad-roach foundations, Hoppers and mole-cricket contributions support complete local projects. Observing release timing or intact contact anatomy can make the same familiar route newly useful when a project changes. Tinwater imports conditioned flight arrays and specialist gland preparations rather than offering every lifecycle in one back room.\n\n**Race tradition.** Short mixed layouts place an ordinary fast run next to one clear passing or recovery opportunity. First Current remains an existing local Open example, not a universal sole gateway. A quiet reference run, a funded qualifying meet and a show evening use different parts of the club programme. An imported kit can compete on its actual merits.\n\n**Accessible project.** Tin Can Special provides a real host and the printed contact/guide edition. A local Hopper conversion is available without requiring a premium lineage. The owner can build and race before choosing whether to inspect a wild source or a breeder’s ledger.\n\n**Economic opportunity.** Stock intake, prepared-contact supply, receiving work, routine fitting and communal-course setup can all earn under actual demand. A common donor buys no guaranteed sale; a customer’s useful ordinary specification is what creates an outlet.\n\n**Faction relationship.** Tinwater is a proposed Rivet Commons member, while Foundry and Two Nets retain their own assets. The Commons can fund shared access; it does not seize Mara’s breeding line or require party membership to purchase a Hopper.\n\n**Local tension and visible objects.** Bench space used for visiting representatives can crowd out regulars; funded extra sessions solve capacity more honestly than secretly cancelling local access. The club board visibly separates booked work, open benches, practice and qualifying races. Visitors see named racers and actual kits, not a wall of progression locks.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S01",
          "W19.S09"
        ],
        "legacy": "LOC_TINWATER",
        "faction": "W19.FACTION.RIVET"
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.REGION.CINDER",
        "name": "Cinderhouse — support under load",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Existing place refined.** Heat recovery, repair workshops and reusable hardware are its ordinary economy. Recovered heat supports work; it is not food or a source of mutation-powered energy. Food, plant matter, Bankseed stock where relevant and ready living materials enter through actual producers and carriers.\n\n**Local hobby.** Cinderhouse Broodworks remains an independent cooperative supplier. The new Loadhouse Club borrows workshop space for racing rather than owning every industrial floor. Members value a construction that works again after transport, fitting and an ordinary rough heat. Some still campaign very light sprint racers; regional identity is not a mandatory heavy-build trait.\n\n**Sources and imports.** Cooling compost and furnace-bed scarabs supply separate force, inner support and outer-protection projects. Bullhorn stock creates contact-shape work. Forceful drive, light supporting plates and protective coverage must be compared independently. Fast-release Glassfoot material and conditioned arrays are sensible imports rather than locally spawned rewards.\n\n**Race tradition.** Boiler Rise, Rivet Wash and long return straights create decisions about loaded starts, contact recovery and how much speed to carry. Every advertised obstacle must offer another plausible response: source quality, fit, stance, reduced unnecessary load or a different legal approach. A Boxbrace is not automatically the answer to a poor landing, and a broad host receives no regional acceleration aura.\n\n**Accessible project.** Foundry Heavy combines an appropriate host, Torque Bundle, suitable Lowstep and actual support. The printed set may retain native contact where adequate. An ordinary complete kit and an expert local reinforcement commission can serve different customers without inventing a rare-gear entrance fee.\n\n**Economic opportunity.** Repeated-dimension plates, customer-stock support work, section repairs and ordinary torque preparations supply everyday business. A premium research line may run beside ordinary accepted stock; neither output is certified by the town’s reputation alone.\n\n**Faction relationship and tension.** The Rivet Commons can call on workshop labor for travelling events. That request competes with water-pump repairs and paid orders. The sporting office pays for a defined replacement shift or books ahead; winning does not confiscate a worker’s stock or leave their dependents’ service unstaffed.\n\n**Material identity.** Open rib samples, load-direction marks and repaired reusable section cases communicate the local specialty. A finished tin records a real prepared support path rather than a generic steel-colored bonus frame.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S02",
          "W19.S08"
        ],
        "legacy": "LOC_CINDER",
        "faction": "W19.FACTION.RIVET"
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.REGION.BASIN",
        "name": "Crown Basin — the stages before the wings",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**New settlement proposal.** Crown Basin is the inhabited service settlement beside the already described Cooling Works ponds and reed margins. This adjacency is new authored geography, not a claim that every earlier Cooling Works patch was part of a mapped municipality. The settlement maintains catchment channels, grows food, repairs water hardware and provides juvenile/emergence space.\n\n**Local hobby.** Crown Loft is a proposed club with an outdoor practice volume, a sheltered introductory loft and an ordinary ground circuit. Inhabitants do not all fly. Ground racers buy contact and reserve stock here, and a new Flyer can buy a ready Skyhook tin rather than own a pond.\n\n**Sources and imports.** Glasswing juveniles and conditioned adults, Longwing reed populations, Clockleaf useful stages and suitable wet-habitat Sumpflies support several distinct products. Water alone is not a productive nursery: prey or plant provisioning, usable stage space and actual reproductive stock are required. Root supports, cases, funds and prepared drives are imported when appropriate.\n\n**Race tradition.** Broad climbing choices, distinct air gates and receiving aprons define the Crown circuit. A ground counterpart crosses a smooth causeway and declared loose inserts; it does not simulate flight by levitating the ground ribbon. Shorter direct aerial climbs and wider gradual climbs can suit different array/drive arrangements without guaranteeing equal speed.\n\n**Accessible project.** Skyhook / Courtyard includes the array, appropriate drive and yoke, supply, control provisions and ordinary receiving support. Longline / Reedfold is the longer forward-flight alternative, not a higher numbered dragonfly. Both show their actual working and carrying shapes.\n\n**Economic opportunity.** Paid grow-out, intact emergence, array matching, stage-aware transport and ordinary conditioned stock give the settlement livelihoods that are not all dependent on a world champion. A failed expansion can leave a reproductively eligible adult; it cannot fulfill an intact-array order by changing its label.\n\n**Faction relationship.** The Reedwater Compact coordinates catchment work and shared facility commitments. Capacity bookings can be denied when genuinely full; the faction does not own a species or award permission to breed it.\n\n**Local tension and objects.** Tournament visitors require support at the same time as developing cohorts. Reserving separate temporary arrival benches avoids emptying productive tanks for guests. Float-level markers, emergence rails, folded-frame cases and broad identification signs make both everyday work and race spectacle visible.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S03",
          "W19.S09"
        ],
        "faction": "W19.FACTION.REED"
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.REGION.SALTGLASS",
        "name": "Saltglass Exchange — good descriptions travel",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Existing place refined.** Trade, glass repair, live-stock packing and careful display support the settlement. Tinted cases, sunshades and legible stock cards distinguish it from a universal rust-brown market. The Saltglass Counter remains a particular broker, not the town government or every resident’s employer.\n\n**Local hobby.** The proposed Lantern Yard venue combines ground racing, an overhead course operated at separate times, and a finishing salon. Preparation samples and ordinary ready tins sit near unusual collector stock. A customer should not need premium ancestry paperwork to buy a dependable contact set.\n\n**Sources and imports.** The Overglass group—Dewglass wall, Hanging Frames and Felt loft—is attached to the surrounding catchment as a proposed regional relationship. Glassfoot, mantis, Ashveil and Sandfelt sources retain separate populations and life histories. Rooted drive tissue, reserve larvae and appropriate hosts arrive through named supply routes; attractive packaging does not make them local captures.\n\n**Race tradition.** Legible narrow/wide line choices and broad overtaking opportunities reward responsive anatomy, while a longer clean run keeps outright pace desirable. Regional Flyer courses use true height and sweep rather than translating every rail into an invisible air wall. A Horn Tax-style build can win here if the actual race permits it; the broker culture grants no foot-release multiplier.\n\n**Accessible project.** Glassline includes contact and useful corrective sensing only where the host has response anatomy. A signature finish is a separate purchase or craft. The same measured ordinary sect may appear in a plain tin and an illustrated local edition without secretly changing its performance.\n\n**Economic opportunity.** Rematching, receiving inspection, appropriate consignment, finishing, documented restoration and exact requested stock are useful services. A visibly rare animal is worth what a real customer values; no city-wide rarity price is asserted.\n\n**Faction relationship and tension.** The Lantern Exchange faction coordinates market-court agreements and road receiving support. A dispute official cannot adjudicate their own shop’s delivery claim or override a race result because a major exhibitor lost. Merchant influence is a source of institutional tension, not a scripted corruption fee every customer must pay.\n\n**Objects.** A repair card separates original wing panels from replacement roots; a counter display separates a natural pattern from applied finish. This accuracy makes products desirable beyond the seller’s acquaintances and gives honest specialists a reason to exist.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S09",
          "W19.S10"
        ],
        "legacy": "LOC_SALTGLASS",
        "faction": "W19.FACTION.LANTERN"
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.REGION.SPANREST",
        "name": "Spanrest — the circuit that arrives in cases",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**New settlement proposal.** Spanrest is a causeway junction with permanent repair households and seasonal crews. Its work is road-surface repair, carrier staging, food provision, reusable packaging and section hardware. Mobile residence is a normal local arrangement, not evidence that somebody lacks the right to participate.\n\n**Local hobby.** The proposed Foldout Club travels with numbered section cases and meets at a fixed receiving yard when crews return. A race can be hosted in a borrowed covered space after the route is properly built; it need not await a stadium. Flat sealed lane panels, supported portals and independently owned kits retain their identities when packed.\n\n**Sources and imports.** Timber-seam and Slab-edge Rivetbacks are associated with maintained verge structures; darkling and mole-cricket source pockets occur where their resources exist. This is an additional locality proposal, not relocation of earlier individuals. Predatory insect colonies need food, and a repaired road is not itself a diet. Delicate wing arrays and useful-stage juveniles are normally received through supported services.\n\n**Race tradition.** A travelling layout changes in published, reproducible ways between meetings. Competitors compare familiar pieces in a new order. An efficient re-fit, sound joint and recoverable landing have value alongside powerful drive. A course builder must provide coherent connectors and real recovery access; unpredictability is not achieved by hiding a broken connector under rust.\n\n**Accessible project.** Tin Can Special, Apron conversions and Right Again are understandable travel projects. A complete Right Again arrangement still requires the host-specific support that lets it work; a recovery hinge is not mandatory insurance sold with every case.\n\n**Economic opportunity.** Packing repair, section lending, route setup, customer-stock adaptation and root-safe transport can support a keep. The Case Runners contract with these crews without becoming identical to the Traverse Freeholds faction. A returned loan is the original section or part, not a newly generated replacement.\n\n**Faction relationship and tension.** The Traverse Freeholds recognize both fixed settlements and registered itinerant households. Their sporting office must accept a supported contact address at a club or carrier yard. The problem of scarce shipping capacity is solved through bookings and support, not a requirement to own land or undertake unrelated courier quests.\n\n**Objects.** Fold marks, paired carrying diagrams, return tags and a repaired timing mast make travel expertise legible. A scuffed kit tin can retain honest history without becoming a higher stat tier.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S08"
        ],
        "faction": "W19.FACTION.TRAVERSE"
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.REGION.CITY",
        "name": "New World City — destination, not the only place that matters",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Author-established destination; new civic detail proposed.** The city hosts the What’s Left of the World Derby. In this working atlas it is a trading and repair metropolis governed locally, not the unquestioned ruler of all factions. It depends on incoming food, living-stock support, specialist production and transport. Hosting the sport does not explain the entire post-winter recovery.\n\n**Two kinds of arrival.** Qualified competitors arrive to represent a faction in their declared discipline. Makers, collectors, workers, spectators and ordinary visiting racers may arrive for the market, displays and public clubs without a Derby berth. Access to the city and access to its championship grid are not the same unlock.\n\n**Local identity.** The spectacle magnifies a bench-scale hobby: named racers travel in supported cases; technicians work at practical benches; crowds see enlarged views and clear course markers. Great public halls coexist with neighborhood hobby rooms. The championship is extraordinary without turning every insect into a rideable war beast.\n\n**Everyday supply.** The city has many customers and higher aggregate demand, but not infinite purses or universal premium prices. A plain prepared part can sell through a routine shop order. A commemorative lid can appeal to a collector without restricting the performance component to a single final-day purchase window.\n\n**Sporting standing.** City-based competitors affiliate through one of the signatory clubs and follow the same ladder. The host municipality has no purchased wildcard or automatically crowned home champion. The title belongs to the valid winner of the final, not to the faction with the largest visiting delegation or most kills.\n\n**Persistent meaning.** Returning from the Derby changes the actual records, possessions and relationships affected. It does not empty the player’s stable, reset their breeding knowledge or make hometown racing obsolete. A world-qualified lanista may still enjoy developing a new ordinary kit alongside a local beginner.\n\n**Institutional areas.** The Receiving Apron, Common Bench Hall, Ground Bowl, Sky Galleries, Counter Ring, Display Walk and Finish House perform separate jobs described later. Their geography remains schematic until an architectural scale plan is approved.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S04",
          "W19.S06"
        ],
        "faction": null
      }
