        "id": "W19.FACTION.RIVET",
        "name": "The Rivet Commons",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Civic foundation.** A compact of pumpwright wards, heat-recovery workshops and trackside households. Tinwater and Cinderhouse are proposed member settlements. Local assemblies manage their own work; elected delegates maintain the shared repairs and travel commitments. This is not the Cinderhouse Broodworks renamed as a state.\n\n**Why the sport matters.** A working construction demonstrates craft that residents recognize, while shared race halls give different occupations a public activity. The Commons values a locally built racer but cannot certify quality from a cooperative stamp. Private builders and imported components remain ordinary parts of the scene.\n\n**Representative institution.** The Rivet Assembly Board organizes the factional final and appoints event staff from clubs that are not involved in the decision under review. Its mandate is sporting administration and funded team support, not ownership of members’ bodies or breeding lines. Selection follows the published race results; an impressive workshop inspection cannot replace the winning race.\n\n**Support package.** A chosen representative receives a contracted bench allowance, replacement shift support where needed, booked outward/return carriage and a bounded stock-maintenance allowance. Goods and services have actual suppliers. No cash or spare anatomy is generated merely by wearing the badge. Personal sponsor additions are recorded separately.\n\n**Construction culture.** Visible ribs, local repair marks, legible root access and reusable tins. Loaded and rough-course expertise is well represented, but the Commons also fields fast narrow racers and complete Flyers. A resident’s choice of natural color or finish is not a political statement unless they make it one.\n\n**Selection occasion.** The Common Bench Final uses a changing multi-layout race programme. Spectators can compare the same host before and after a permitted refit. The demonstration benches beside it are not scoring stations. The winner earns the seat through valid racing.\n\n**Internal pressure.** Workshop time devoted to elite preparation competes with communal maintenance. The public budget separates required repairs, local hobby access and representative support. This creates commissions and debates without an obligatory sabotage storyline or a hidden faction tax on all stock.\n\n**Relations.** Reedwater supplies biological grow-out; Lantern brokers difficult components; Traverse crews provide transport and setup. The Commons exports useful support preparations and hardware. Members can cooperate on a product and compete fiercely on the track.\n\n**Emblem and objects.** Three offset rib shapes joined by a small circular fastener mark. Printed on cases and banners, not burned into specimens as an ownership requirement. The most characteristic object is an annotated working kit guide beside its still-owned reference racer.",
