      {
        "id": "W19.FACTION.LANTERN",
        "name": "The Lantern Exchange",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Civic foundation.** A charter association of market wards, packers, artisans and trade-road settlements. Saltglass Exchange is its reference center. Membership is not restricted to merchants; repair workers, caregivers and customers have civic standing. The Saltglass Counter remains one private business among several.\n\n**Why the sport matters.** A recognizable racer moves knowledge and demand between communities. The faction sponsors both competitive events and exhibitions because these reveal different forms of craft. An excellent public result can prompt an inquiry, while a beautiful display can be valuable without winning a race.\n\n**Representative institution.** The Lantern Race Exchange publishes selection conditions and separates judges from commercial interests in the affected entrants. Donor assays and delivery evidence are useful, but the factional berth follows race results. A buyer cannot purchase the berth by buying the winning host or paying a judge’s employer.\n\n**Support package.** Funded receiving, approved fitting work, shipment space and return care can accompany a representative. Sponsors may request public maker or supplier credit; they cannot demand a hidden equipment restriction contrary to the entered Open class. A representative may decline the commercial offer while retaining their already-earned sporting qualification and seek another legitimate support route.\n\n**Construction culture.** Clear windows, labeled parts, restrained printed livery and exact edition names. Complex and simple kits coexist. A commemorative product can use ordinary excellent stock; a plain crate can contain a valuable specimen. Honest substitutes and well-described custom fit are part of the local craft identity.\n\n**Selection occasion.** The Lantern Exchange Final takes place beside a separately judged display salon. Neither score carries into the other. The race programme tests actual contact, approach and air/ground route behavior. Judges cannot award racing positions for an elegant tin or a popular publication.\n\n**Internal pressure.** Large suppliers want guaranteed exposure; independent makers want fair access. A rotating exhibitor roster and separately allocated public bench spaces address capacity. Exceptional attention never becomes an automatic genome buff, unlimited order or universal price multiplier.\n\n**Relations.** The Exchange imports from all three other factions and sells many of their products onward. Its disputes with them concern practical promises and transport responsibility, not an assumption that outsiders’ components are prohibited. Shared measurements reduce the cost of comparing unfamiliar stock.\n\n**Emblem and objects.** A shaded lamp outline above two opposing case handles. The factional sash is detachable; a natural shell pattern does not become counterfeit livery. The characteristic object is a paired product card: the construction’s actual contents on one face, its honestly supported provenance on the other.",
