      {
        "id": "W19.FACTION.TRAVERSE",
        "name": "The Traverse Freeholds",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Civic foundation.** A federation of fixed roadside households, mobile repair crews and settlement-to-settlement support yards. Spanrest is its reference receiving node. The political compact maintains passage infrastructure and negotiated stopping rights. It does not claim every caravan or the independent Case Runners as members.\n\n**Why the sport matters.** A few well-packed section cases can bring a real meeting to a place with no permanent track. Showing up with a working racer and sharing a course is a recognizable civic act. Travelling builders develop distinctive constructions because preparation, repeat fitting and preservation matter, not because distance grants a handling stat.\n\n**Representative institution.** The Traverse Circuit Office records mobile club contacts, recognized results and the moving final’s published location. A competitor can use a club or receiving yard as a contact point. No land deed, permanent address, employer sponsorship or carrier career is required for sporting affiliation.\n\n**Support package.** Reserved case space, setup labor, a shared tool allowance and return carriage are real commitments. A selected entrant need not carry unrelated freight as a compulsory quest to redeem their ticket. Extra voluntary carrier work remains a separate livelihood with its own quote.\n\n**Construction culture.** Repairable cases, retained native anatomy where useful, sectional supports, visible return paths and easily inspected working clearances. A quick lightweight kit can travel well; a substantial Open monster can also be carried with suitable support. 'Portable' is not a universal small-part whitelist.\n\n**Selection occasion.** The Moving Final announces its layout, rules and venue before entrants commit. Changing the location after registration requires a practical rebooking or renewed qualifier opportunity; it does not secretly reward people who were near an unannounced stop. In-world travel creates decisions, while real-world absence remains paused by default.\n\n**Internal pressure.** Road work and race traffic compete for the same crews and cases. The office contracts enough capacity for earned delegates before promising free journeys. Delays are handled through actual rerouting or timetable revision, not a teleporting case or a blank new funding pool.\n\n**Relations.** The Freeholds sell useful carriage and construction access to the other factions. They buy stage-specific support and workshop work in return. Their champions can use imported premium anatomy and remain recognizably Traverse through the preparation, record and community that supported them.\n\n**Emblem and objects.** Two staggered chevrons passing through an open square. Numbered case corners and portable course diagrams give the mark a material context. The characteristic trophy travels in the original repaired transit case; later copies are labeled reproductions.",
