    "support": [
      {
        "id": "W19.SUPPORT.CYCLE",
        "name": "The festival creates demand without manufacturing scarcity",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Course publication.** Clubs, fitters and suppliers see what types of running, contact and air movement will actually be useful. No course is altered secretly to sell one supplier’s exclusive counter.\n\n**Preparation period.** Existing lines can supply ordinary measurements; specialists offer a missing contribution or adaptation. Price pressure requires a real buyer, quantity and supply cause. A new poster does not instantly multiply every ask in every town.\n\n**Qualification and travel.** The event pays its agreed prizes and the representative programme books the promised support. Spare-stock loans state actual return obligations. No qualifier badge buys unrecorded extra insects.\n\n**The festival.** Display, racing, kit building, source trading and ordinary social attendance coexist. Most transactions can remain aggregated at a stated market boundary; important recurring customers preserve individual products and histories.\n\n**Return.** A racer, maker, breeder or collector brings back owned objects, records, observations and actual paid or offered work. A published win may prompt a request; it does not certify a whole line or create an already-settled sale. Back home, ordinary clubs continue.\n\n**Another season.** New legitimate events, layouts and work renew interest. Rare appearance stock can be desirable, but its availability is not tied to real-world login streaks. Historical editions remain historical; a revival identifies what was newly produced.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S04",
          "W19.S10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.SUPPORT.MEET",
        "name": "A meeting budget must buy the meeting",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "An event offer separates host work, participant costs, prizes and travel support. The host quotes section use, setup, timing, receiving, ordinary care, venue use and staffing. The purse is a separate funded commitment. A promised travel floor is another commitment, not an amount the winner must extract from the prize after the event.\n\nSponsorship, exhibitors, participant fees, public support and ticket income are possible sources when actually authored. Expected crowd size is not cash already available. Each promised service has a responsible provider or funded contingency before confirmation. A small supported club meet can be legitimate without commercial ticket revenue.\n\nSetup crews, fitters and nursery services can earn around the meeting, but participants are not compelled into those jobs. A breeder who dislikes racing can supply accepted ordinary stock; a racer who dislikes breeding can buy it. Prize money is not the sole source of income in the world.\n\nNo price table is added here merely to make this sector appear profitable. W16/W17 budget examples remain conditional and unchanged. Later matched comparisons must include care during travel, unsold inventory, hired labor, actual event renewal and the source of customer funds. An unpaid advertisement or a displayed trophy cannot settle those bills.",
