      {
        "id": "W19.HISTORY.BRIDGEJOINT",
        "name": "Bridgejoint: an ordinary service pattern becomes a recognized product",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**H27 / working example.** Len Voss fits a customer’s existing Lowstep using separately identified lining material. The useful achievement is freely repeated travel within that customer’s geometry, not an improved ratio. The customer keeps the host; Len keeps a permitted preparation record. No racing win is invented to justify the product.\n\n**H28 / first guide.** Bridgejoint 1 prints a specific supported motion range and explains whether sleeves are supplied separately or already included in the hip assembly. Its case is an open-sided motion frame, not a clamp that arrests the joint. Multiple real donors can satisfy the preparation.\n\n**H30 / second printed pattern.** Bridgejoint 2 adds a second fitted geometry and a clearer grit-service interval. The number is a preparation/guide revision, not generation count or automatically superior genes. Edition 1 components remain useful where their geometry works; no global obsolescence is declared.\n\n**Objects.** W19.HERITAGE.BJ_GUIDE1 is an archival annotated guide. W19.HERITAGE.BJ_CASE2 is a new edition’s product-case pattern. They are not living stock. A later manufactured set identifies its own sources and does not acquire the first customer’s anatomy or history by using the name.\n\n**Demand.** Travelling fitters and ordinary kit makers can buy known dimensional suitability. A collector may separately want the marked guide. Any working or collector price requires the actual buyer’s purpose. No faction-exclusive stock or logo multiplier is introduced.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S10"
        ],
        "maker": "W19.PERSON.LEN",
        "authority": "new_fictional_history_not_observed_game_event"
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.HISTORY.LANTERNTHREAD",
        "name": "Lantern Thread: a pattern line and two kinds of display",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**H26 / observed appearance.** Eska’s project begins with two separately recorded Ashveil founders showing the desired vein-and-band relation. Their apparent symmetry is a field observation, not a guarantee of offspring or aero performance. Parentage uncertainty is retained where present.\n\n**H28 / repeated selected display stock.** Recorded descendants across broods supply a bounded appearance claim. Some useful adults remain in the line. Specimens prepared into vanes are marked as such; retained parents are not simultaneously harvested. The first living display case presents the actual animal and its current stage.\n\n**H30 / printed display edition.** Lantern Thread / Veil card offers either a living specimen or a clearly labeled prepared display surface. Those are separate listings with different care and provenance. A finished racing kit can combine selected patterned material with another measured component source, provided the supplied contents and functional promises are honest.\n\n**Objects.** W19.HERITAGE.LT_LEDGER is the archived observation record. W19.HERITAGE.LT_VEIL_CARD is the contemporary edition pattern. Their existence does not prove the early founders are alive, create a line-wide speed trait or assign all similar natural patterns to Eska.\n\n**Demand.** A collector seeks the natural design; a racer may seek a measured surface regardless of color; a finisher composes the selected material with the host’s actual silhouette. A display award belongs to the exhibited work, not automatically to every descendant from the line.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S04",
          "W19.S10"
        ],
        "maker": "W19.PERSON.ESKA",
        "authority": "new_fictional_history_not_observed_game_event"
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.HISTORY.RETURNEDHORN",
        "name": "Returned Horn: original core, replaced working interface",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**H24 / original assembly.** A newly authored record identifies horn core RH-1 in a club demonstration build. Its role is a recorded construction example; it is not retroactively assigned a world victory. The donor and former host histories remain separate.\n\n**H29 / material failure.** A receiving record finds the structural core sound but the working interface no longer serviceable. The core can remain an original object. A display does not need to conceal the loss of working readiness, and filling a reserve cannot restart a failed root.\n\n**H30 / restoration.** An independent fitter supplies a new identified interface and performs the agreed support/fit check. The restored component retains RH-1 as its original core and records the new material separately. It can be used on a different eligible host without inheriting that host’s future races in advance.\n\n**Objects.** W19.HERITAGE.RH_CORE1 is an actual historical-core concept; W19.HERITAGE.RH_RESTORE30 is the restoration record. A contemporary 'Returned Horn style' tin contains another newly sourced horn, not another original RH-1.\n\n**Demand.** The restorer earns for useful work. A collector may pay for the supported history; a competitor may value the working geometry. Neither demand requires the two values to be equal. Ordinary examples with the same effective geometry are allowed to race just as well.",
        "refs": [
          "W19.S10"
        ],
        "authority": "new_fictional_history_not_observed_game_event"
      }
    ],
    "scope": [
      {
        "id": "W19.SCOPE.SCALE",
        "name": "Preferred scale-study envelope — not adopted world measurements",
        "status": "proposal_not_adopted",
        "body": "**Decision status.** Absolute scale remains open author authority. This volume recommends a concrete comparison frame for later art/logic studies while leaving D02 unresolved in the preserved records. It is a proposal to test, not a statement that every previously named animal has these dimensions.\n\n| Candidate | Proposed native host body length | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| S0 / very small | 30–50 mm | Dense home circuits and compact cases; anatomy and display may require more magnification. |\n| S1 / preferred study frame | 70–100 mm | Recognizable roots and limbs at a bench, still hand-carried; broad deployed arrays require larger shaped cases. |\n| S2 / large pet-scale | 200–300 mm | More costly housing, carrying and venue footprint; risks displacing the small-kit identity toward large animal handling. |\n\nS1 is preferred because the kit can remain hand-built and personally carried while individual anatomical mechanisms can be recognized. It does not set a sporting size ceiling. Donor anatomy, additional limbs, horn projection and working wing sweep can extend beyond the body-length range. Those extensions need actual case and course clearance, not an invented universal miniaturization field.\n\nA later dimensioned study must put the same host beside an adult hand, a tray, a complete tin, a home course, a crowded pack section and an aerial gate. The route must accommodate the intended field rather than simply multiplying body length by a decorative coefficient. Numbers chosen for a prototype board do not supply this evidence.\n\nUntil the study is adopted, W19 locations use relative working relationships and route adjacency. No travel fare, housing capacity, collider or lifespan is derived from S1. The final teams can retrieve the recommendation without mistaking it for settled canon.",
        "refs": [],
        "adoption_required": true
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.SCOPE.RECOVERY",
        "name": "Two candidate directions for the post-winter account",
        "status": "proposal_not_adopted",
        "body": "The original premise gives roaches exceptional survival while the current hobby requires people, donor insects, food webs and materials. The account of how that world returned is still an author-level choice. W19 names alternatives instead of quietly copying an earlier vault story into history.\n\n**R1 / continuous surface lineage, recommended for review.** Rad roaches were the only insects maintaining a continuous widespread active surface lineage through the full winter. Other life persisted in restricted refuges, dormant stages or maintained collections and re-expanded afterward. This is a proposed interpretation of the strong survival premise, not proof that the phrase always meant this. It explains why the roach is a universal adaptable host while donor diversity can be ecological and regional.\n\n**R2 / deliberate restoration.** A narrower surviving roach biosphere is later enriched through protected stock and deliberate reintroduction. This can support dramatic recovery histories, but requires an independently authored restoration capability, actual founders and food-web support. It must not turn present-day access to ordinary insects into a mandatory faction research license.\n\n**Shared requirements.** Present inhabitants need food, water, energy and care. Heat and scrap provide shelter or useful work, not calories. Predatory donors need prey; stage-dependent production needs its actual developmental inputs. The sports calendar is thirty years after the founders, not automatically thirty years after the bombs.\n\n**No silent selection.** The regional livelihoods in this volume state what supports them now without deciding R1 or R2 as past truth. The preferred proposal remains clearly identified for author review. Broad death, aging and regeneration are not settled by either history, and material service does not grant unlimited copies of rare anatomy.",
        "refs": [],
        "adoption_required": true
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.SCOPE.CULTURE",
        "name": "A regional difference has a mechanism",
        "status": "proposal_not_adopted",
        "body": "A meaningful regional variant changes one or more of: source availability, field evidence, developmental services, accepted product purpose, preparation practice, course geometry, local relationships, visible craft or transport access. A name and color alone are not sufficient.\n\nA meaningful faction difference changes ordinary organization, shared resources, representation, public priorities and the way support is provided. It is not a passive stat package. A faction should contain internal disagreement and several kinds of hobbyist without requiring a narrative chapter to justify every transaction.\n\nA meaningful later championship changes opponents, commitments, course repertoire, audience and stakes. It must not quietly multiply an NPC’s drive, read the player’s hidden genes or remove supported counterplay. The same useful part still means the same anatomy at a local club and the world final.\n\nThe full course and tissue simulations remain downstream. This reference defines conditions a developer must represent and examples of alternative responses; it does not claim that every stated construction is already balanced or effective in an executable engine.",
        "refs": [],
        "adoption_required": true
      }
    ],
    "objects": [
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.PASSPORT",
        "name": "Two-layer competition passport",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** A durable host record sits beside a replaceable campaign entry sleeve. The animal’s name and image stay prominent; lanista, discipline, affiliation and edition are separate fields.\n\n**Hobby use.** The old winner can be sold without selling the former owner’s berth. A different host can join the next meeting without rewriting the previous race.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** No genotype exposure, title transfer or extra living animal created by the document.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.ROSTER",
        "name": "Delegation sash and roster board",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** A detachable faction sash is shown beside the named person and actual host; a separate line lists crew, loaned stock and the standby.\n\n**Hobby use.** The visitor can see who represents whom, and which parts are borrowed, without implying all members of a species belong to that faction.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** One historical win cannot authorize several simultaneous delegations.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.ROADCASE",
        "name": "Outbound-and-return racing case",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** Supports for the actual packed state, a destination and receiving contact, an ordinary support allowance and a visible return booking.\n\n**Hobby use.** Preparation accounts for both journeys; extra spare stock consumes real space. The case can remain a cherished object after the campaign.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** The outbound ticket is not a storage-life reset or a compulsory courier mission.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.COURSECARD",
        "name": "Regional course and opportunity card",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** An overhead/side layout schematic, piece identities, actual rough/loose/air sections, route choices, finish and retrieval boundaries.\n\n**Hobby use.** Entrants can notice useful contexts for their existing components and see why another approach might work.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** A colored region cannot secretly activate an item or stand in for an unbuilt bridge.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.DIRECTORY",
        "name": "Club-service directory leaf",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** A clean listing distinguishes shop stock, benches, nursery stages, course type, public sessions and supported receiving.\n\n**Hobby use.** A novice can find a ready kit; a specialist can find a matching bench. Not every shop is assumed to house every service.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** No universal real-time stock oracle or invented same-day transport availability.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.STEWARDS",
        "name": "Inspection tag with change record",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** A small numbered tag identifies the accepted host configuration and the next allowed service window. A correction strip can show a resolved discrepancy.\n\n**Hobby use.** Officials inspect the actual preparation; spectators can relate it to the next frozen race entry.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** No universal certification of genetic quality or permission to prohibit creative Open builds by brand.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.EDITION",
        "name": "Regional edition lid and contents card",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** The lid has a regional maker mark and recognizable complete construction. The card identifies supplied host, source preparations, native roles and optional substitutions.\n\n**Hobby use.** Appearance and product identity can vary without concealing a required component or pretending a decorative badge improves performance.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** Do not clone the reference champion or remove the recipient’s assembly step.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.RESULT",
        "name": "Finish strip and Benchline correction",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** A large result strip carries winner and placing before optional technical context. An attached dated correction identifies a changed observation or adjudication.\n\n**Hobby use.** The world can remember and revise a claim without erasing the original event or fabricating certainty.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** Audience attention is not already-earned revenue.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.DISPLAY",
        "name": "Heritage plinth with material map",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** A modest raised case shows the real object alongside a sketch of original core, later replacements and applied finish.\n\n**Hobby use.** Original material, descendant stock, restored working component and replica have honest separate identities.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** A beautiful piece does not require race power; a reproduction is not fraud when labeled.",
        "refs": []
      },
      {
        "id": "W19.OBJECT.WORK",
        "name": "Support-offer sheet",
        "status": "authored_working_definition",
        "body": "**Physical form.** One face shows the promised travel, care, bench and return services; the reverse identifies actual providers and optional extras.\n\n**Hobby use.** An earned seat comes with an intelligible practical route. Specialists can inspect obligations without requiring every player to manage a dozen contracts.\n\n**Misreading to prevent.** No empty sponsorship meter substituted for actual funded capacity.",
        "refs": []
      }
    ],
    "amendments": [
